.TH SCENEFS 4
.SH NAME
scenefs \- a live 2-D scene with a shared camera and a stream of its changes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount {scenefs
[
.B -D
]
.B }
.I mountpoint
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Scenefs
serves a scene: things in a plane or on the globe, over time.
Producers write entities, features and layers; viewers such as
.IR scene (1)
read them; anyone steers the shared camera through
.BR ctl .
Every change is also a record on the
.B changes
stream, so recording a run is reading that file, and playing one back is
.I scenereplay
(see
.IR scene (1))
writing it into
.BR log .
.I Scenefs
keeps no history of its own.
It serves 9P on its standard input; the conventional mount point is
.BR /mnt/scene .
.PP
.B -D
traces 9P messages on standard error.
.PP
The data model \(em frames, the stanza attributes, the record grammar \(em
is
.BR docs/scene-design.md .
Stanza files hold one
.IB attr = value
per line.
.SH FILESYSTEM
.TF entities/
.TP
.B ctl
write-only, one command per line:
.BI center " a b" ,
.BI zoom " z" ,
.BR fit ,
.BI follow " id" ,
.BR unfollow ,
.BI select " id" ,
.BR deselect ;
.BR clear .
Positions are in the scene's frame: latitude and longitude for
.BR frame=geo ,
x and y for
.BR frame=xy .
.TP
.B view
the shared camera:
.BI "frame " "f " "center " "a b " "zoom " "z " "sel " "id " "follow " "id " "fit " n\f1,
with
.B -
for none.
.I N
is a pending fit request, 0 when there is none: the first viewer to see
it fits the data to its own size and writes the camera back, which
clears it.
A fresh scene starts with a request.
.TP
.B status
.BI "t " "t " "gen " "g"
and the entity, feature and layer counts.
.I Gen
changes whenever the scene changes, so a viewer polls this one file.
.TP
.B event
read blocks until something happens and returns one line:
.BI "gen " g ,
.BI "select " id
or
.BI "view " ... ;
each open is its own cursor.
Newer
.B gen
and
.B view
events replace queued ones.
.TP
.B changes
read blocks until the scene changes and returns the change as records,
each preceded by a
.B time
record when the clock has moved.
Each open is its own cursor, and begins with the scene as it stands
(a
.B clear
and the records that rebuild it), so what one open reads is a
self-contained recording.
Nothing is dropped; a reader that falls more than 4MB behind is cut off
with an error.
.TP
.B log
write-only: records, one per line
.RB ( "time" ,
.BR ent ,
.BR feat ,
.BR layer ,
.BR meta ,
.BR del ,
.BR clear ).
Viewers see a write whole; a bad record stops it with an error, and the
records before it stand.
.TP
.B meta
the scene's stanza
.RB ( frame= ,
.BR projection= ,
.BR units= ,
.BR title= ,
.BR trail= ,
.BR bounds= ).
.TP
.B time
the scene clock.
.TP
.BI entities/ id
.PD 0
.TP
.BI features/ id
.TP
.BI layers/ id
.PD
one stanza file per object.
Create, write and remove them as ordinary files; a write lands at its
offset and takes effect at once, so
.B echo >
and
.B echo >>
do what they say.
.PD
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
mkdir -p /mnt/scene
mount -c {scenefs} /mnt/scene
echo 'meta frame=xy units=m ''title=Test''' > /mnt/scene/log
echo 'time 0' > /mnt/scene/log
echo 'ent r1 x=0 y=0 shape=triangle group=blue course=45' > /mnt/scene/log
cat /mnt/scene/entities/r1
echo 'follow r1' > /mnt/scene/ctl
cat /mnt/scene/changes > /tmp/run.scene &
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/scenefs.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/scene.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR scene (1),
.IR scene (2),
.B docs/scene-design.md
